WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.820
- I'm calling you right now
from a Windows 10 computer.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.360
What is gonna be different when I upgrade

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:06.340
to Windows 11?

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.450
- I would say the experience, right?

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.820
It's modern and more importantly,
it's centered on you.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.430
Even just that little subtle shift

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.363
of the start in the center,

00:00:17.363 --> 00:00:21.510
centered, centered, centered,
centered, centered around you.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.293
- [Joanna] There it is, from
the king of Microsoft himself.

00:00:24.293 --> 00:00:27.530
In Windows 11, the company's
next big operating system

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:28.810
due out later this year,

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.399
the start button moves
to the bottom center.

00:00:31.399 --> 00:00:32.740
(happy music)

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:35.740
But that cosmetic change
actually has deeper meaning.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:36.630
The word.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:37.463
- Center.

00:00:37.463 --> 00:00:39.380
- [Joanna] Is core to
Satya Nadella's vision

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:41.460
that Windows 11 should be at the center

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.870
of your digital life

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.850
as Apple and Google entice users

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.160
to increasingly closed off worlds.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.800
He wants Windows 11 to
be a compelling choice

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.460
for people who don't wanna
live exclusively in either.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.790
- Windows has a role to play

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.230
as that most open of ecosystems today

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:00.930
that plays nicely with,
in fact, the others.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.998
We want you to be able
to connect your phones

00:01:02.998 --> 00:01:04.880
to your PCs.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.880
We want you to be able to
communicate to people on phones

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.390
or other PCs.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.020
- [Joanna] The proof is in the features.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.820
Windows 11 can now run Android apps.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:15.390
To learn more about the new software,

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:17.380
Microsoft gave me an exclusive interview

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.330
with the company's CEO and chairman.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:20.940
I know there's a new start sound

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.652
and I wanted to know if you
could do an impression of it.

00:01:23.652 --> 00:01:26.310
- I wish, I need more talent.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.230
(lively Windows music)

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.280
- I took away three
things the company's doing

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:31.670
in this new operating system

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.170
to lure users and app makers.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.170
Number one, a new new modern interface.

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:39.310
Those who remember Windows
8 know that messing

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.143
with the Start menu

00:01:40.143 --> 00:01:43.300
before didn't work out
so well for Microsoft.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.090
There's not somebody's job at Microsoft

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.340
which is how should we
move the Start button,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.350
where should we move it?

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:49.780
- I'm sure it is as well

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.640
and we'll always have also have the option

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.410
with Windows as you know,

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:54.870
we'll always have the option for anybody

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.550
to go back to whatever
classic mode they want.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.100
- [Joanna] But the placement is only part

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.310
of the fresh Start.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:01.470
Click the button itself

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:02.640
and you get your apps

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:05.010
but also cloud-connected recommendations

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.890
of what you might need next.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.803
- The design system of Windows

00:02:08.803 --> 00:02:11.740
is about you getting to your apps,

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.603
your files, the people you
wanna connect with faster,

00:02:15.603 --> 00:02:17.780
with less friction.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.180
- There are lots of other interface

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.550
and design changes.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.080
This button on the taskbar
brings up a widget screen

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.700
where you can view all sorts of news

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.880
and quick glance information.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.700
Hover on the maximize window
button and you get a tool

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.420
to help you place multiple
windows side by side.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.310
These screenshots provided by Microsoft

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.190
were taken on pre-release software

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.200
and the features are not final.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.600
Tell me, what kind of
role did the pandemic play

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.930
in the development of Windows 11?

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.800
- We definitely were influenced

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:49.590
by the need for a modern operating system.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.609
In some sense, the
original idea of Windows

00:02:52.609 --> 00:02:55.540
has suddenly become, again in 2021,

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:56.700
a huge priority.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:58.466
- [Joanna] One of those
pandemic-driven features,

00:02:58.466 --> 00:03:00.580
a universal mute button

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.980
that lives in the system tray

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.370
so you don't always have to go digging

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:04.740
in whatever video calling app

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.170
to mute yourself.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:07.273
I'm very into it.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.660
The second thing, a new
store with Android apps.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.410
- We're gonna welcome all apps

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:16.220
across even all the different
application platforms

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.990
in Windows itself.

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:19.870
It can be a PWP or a web app.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.160
It can be a native app

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.380
and we're gonna support
Android as first class as well.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.120
- All right, tech translation.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.070
Up until now the Microsoft Store

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.453
was just for certain
kinds of Windows apps.

00:03:31.453 --> 00:03:32.286
(text buzzing)

00:03:32.286 --> 00:03:35.150
Now it will be open for any Windows app

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:36.480
but the real headline,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.960
Microsoft is partnering with
Amazon's Android App Store.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:40.880
Tell me a little bit about

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.400
what you're doing with the Microsoft Store

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.830
on Windows 10 and specifically there,

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.160
what you're doing with Amazon.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.710
- In fact, you can go to our store,

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.380
search, find an Android application

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:52.877
and then you'll be linked
into the Amazon Store

00:03:52.877 --> 00:03:55.470
and you can use the Amazon Store apps

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:56.700
as if they're Windows apps

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.970
and all of the windowing will work.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:03:59.940
- [Joanna] It's a big move

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.060
and one clearly aimed at Chromebooks,

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.340
which also run Android apps.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:09.260
In the last year, unit sales
of Chromebooks grew by 275%.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.910
Windows 11 will also
enable more Windows apps

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.470
to run on new laptops and desktops powered

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.350
by ARM mobile chips from Qualcomm.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.670
- If you think about the traditional role

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:20.620
of operating systems,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:23.050
operating systems are very much needed

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.150
whenever there is a real change
in the system architecture.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:29.330
I mean, you think about the AI chips now,

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.680
it's not just the CPU and the GPU

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.300
but now MPUs are all
becoming part of any PC.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.540
- Another translation.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.000
All of these parts of the chip set

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.610
have changed in big ways
in the last few years

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.910
to make computers smarter
and more power efficient.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.740
Windows 11 will work better with them.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.340
And that brings us to the
third: a focus on openness.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:52.430
Why Windows?

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:56.310
Why should someone get Windows
over Chrome, over macOS?

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.390
- Windows gives you the
most choice of systems,

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:03.610
to apps, to everything that
you wanna be able to do.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:05.660
I think Windows, in fact,

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.640
is becoming increasingly the device

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:11.152
that bridges all of these ecosystems.

00:05:11.152 --> 00:05:11.985
- Do you think of yourself

00:05:11.985 --> 00:05:14.450
as sort of the Switzerland
there of the ecosystems?

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:16.640
- I think that more than
when we say Switzerland,

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.980
I think there are certain platform rules

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:22.500
by which we principally drive Windows.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.240
I'm not saying that other ecosystems

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.590
shouldn't have their own platform rules.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.120
We have a different set of platform rules

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.049
and those platform
rules, I believe, in 2021

00:05:30.049 --> 00:05:33.230
are more needed than they ever were.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:36.270
- How are your platform rules
different than say Apple?

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.044
- One simple thing, we have the ability

00:05:39.044 --> 00:05:42.600
to have multiple
marketplaces that can thrive.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.300
We wanna have a great marketplace

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.620
but we also welcome other marketplaces.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.950
We wanna have great tools like say Teams,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.020
but we also welcome Zoom or Slack

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.330
or anything else to be also first class.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.330
- Apple's iOS specifically does not allow

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:57.890
for third-party app stores

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:00.760
and has been the focus of
recent anti-trust scrutiny.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.040
So if you have an Android phone,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.712
it works great with Windows.

00:06:03.712 --> 00:06:05.700
What about the iPhone?

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.270
- We would love to make sure

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:08.500
that it works better.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.030
I mean, we do everything we can.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:11.810
Like anything that Apple wants to do

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:14.620
with Windows, whether
it's iTunes or iMessage

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.409
or what have you, we would welcome that.

00:06:17.409 --> 00:06:20.620
But overall, we wanna make
sure our software runs great

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:22.046
on Apple devices

00:06:22.046 --> 00:06:25.240
and Windows works well with any software

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:27.150
from anyone, whether it's Google or Apple

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:28.490
or Adobe or anyone.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:31.120
- But what Mr. Nadella's
biggest challenge seems to be,

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:32.960
moving ahead without alienating those

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.510
who love Windows of yore.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.060
I wanna read you something you said

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:38.160
in your first event as Microsoft CEO

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.560
and this was in 2014.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.350
You said, "This business
does not respect tradition."

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:46.090
- What it respects is innovation
on a go-forward basis.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:49.071
So it's really our collective challenge

00:06:49.071 --> 00:06:52.970
that we now need to make Microsoft thrive

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:55.700
in a mobile-first and a cloud-first world.

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.868
- How do you innovate
with avoiding a disaster

00:06:57.868 --> 00:06:59.180
like Windows 8?

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:01.290
- It's all about the batting average

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.400
at the end of it all.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.160
The fact that here 35
years after its inception

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.380
and there's no innovation
that's risk free.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:10.350
So let's say that.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:14.030
And then boldly go forward.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:14.863
- But don't worry,

00:07:14.863 --> 00:07:16.060
you can still move your start button

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:17.170
to where it's always been.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.900
11, it's a big number.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.670
It's a lucky number, 11 is a lucky number?

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:22.870
- Oh, it is a lucky number.

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:24.530
Two ones make 11.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:26.090
- Two ones make 11.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:27.568
Also add up to two.

00:07:27.568 --> 00:07:29.268
(Satya chuckling)
- There you go.

